The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
while (i--) {
    liquidio_destroy_nic_device(octeon_dev, i);
}
```
and the missing decrement before:
```c
goto setup_nic_dev_free;
```
in the devlink_alloc failure path.

Reason:  
The `while (i--)` loop decrements `i` before executing the body, so it skips the current failing index (the one that caused the jump). Additionally, when `devlink_alloc` fails, `i` is not decremented before the goto, causing the last successfully allocated device (at index `i-1`) to be leaked. Together, these off-by-one errors result in a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
do {
    liquidio_destroy_nic_device(octeon_dev, i);
} while (i--);
```
and the added decrement:
```c
i--;
goto setup_nic_dev_free;
```
in the devlink_alloc failure path.

Reason:  
The `do { ... } while (i--);` loop executes the body first, then decrements `i`, so it correctly cleans up the current index `i` and all previously allocated indices. Adding `i--` before the goto in the `devlink_alloc` failure ensures that `i` points to the last successfully allocated device (since the loop index `i` was already incremented after the last successful allocation). This fixes the off-by-one errors and prevents memory leaks.

